<p>The World Health Organization on Sunday declared the Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of Congo and Uganda a public health emergency of international concern, citing cross-border spread, rising case reports and uncertainty over the scale of transmission. The WHO said the outbreak is caused by the Bundibugyo virus and does not meet the criteria for a pandemic emergency. The WHO cited eight laboratory-confirmed cases, 246 suspected cases and 80 suspected deaths in DRC&#x27;s Ituri province as of Saturday, while Africa CDC had reported 336 suspected cases and 88 deaths by Saturday. Health authorities reported infections in Bunia, Rwampara and Mongbwalu, a confirmed case in Kinshasa in a person who had returned from Ituri, and two confirmed cases in Uganda, including one death. The WHO said the outbreak presents a risk to neighboring countries and advised against closing international borders. The agency also said the Bundibugyo virus has no approved strain-specific vaccine or treatment.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/lGXGVF6b_rTZyGpMo32HeSQ_V7WbDno4BywyUK8jxOKV_MVhCixnpaPSVDTKrj_BwvICivAt3PD2r7xJ9NybySCWLYhDvFfV-zqbOmxM2IevBHf6Ub3pGhxr5RFCMeBbpR24XNSp51Dtlie8sGEpeRB0H-TsFm4hVRiPFt8fxB91jM9-rcAokUA3k0_xBco87txEMyOlk5GXBGL8KvI9inVWuhtXmfQabXGbPuk48m9NQoAEGkz7mvNqAPRDuHq1Cy-Ug9yAoexD95MPgw' alt='Staff members at CBCA Virunga Hospital prepare rooms intended for possible suspected Ebola cases in Goma, Democratic Republic of Congo, on May 17, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Possible wider spread: WHO said suspected cases had also been reported in North Kivu, one of Congo&#x27;s most populous provinces and a neighbor of Ituri.</li><li>Health worker risk: ICIR reported infections among health workers as one factor behind WHO&#x27;s emergency declaration.</li><li>Americans exposed: CBS News cited sources saying at least six Americans in Congo had been exposed to Ebola, while SCMP cited a STAT report saying several U.S. citizens had high-risk exposures.</li><li>Containment obstacles: WHO cited insecurity, humanitarian conditions, high population mobility and informal health facilities in eastern DRC as factors increasing spread risk.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>World Health Organization: WHO convened governments, partners and technical agencies to coordinate response priorities and preparedness in affected and neighboring countries. (<a href='https://allafrica.com/stories/202605180077.html'>AllAfrica</a>)</li><li>Ugandan government: Ugandan officials said a 59-year-old Congolese man who died Thursday tested positive, and his body had been returned to DRC. (<a href='https://www.graphic.com.gh/international/international-news/who-declares-ebola-outbreak-in-dr-congo-a-global-health-emergency.html'>Graphic Online</a>)</li><li>U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention: The CDC planned to send additional staff to affected countries and provide technical support through its country offices. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/united-states/us-cdc-to-escalate-ebola-response-after-who-declares-emergency'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/5/18/aje-onl-nf_cllip-uganda-responds-with-measures-to-ebola-180526'>Uganda responds with emergency measures to Ebola outbreak</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/americans-ebola-congo-outbreak-cdc/'>At least 6 Americans in Congo were exposed to Ebola virus, sources say</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/05/17/world/africa/ebola-congo-uganda-who-public-health-emergency.html'>W.H.O. Declares Ebola Outbreak a Global Health Emergency</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-ebola-outbreak-with-88-suspected-deaths-in-congo-declared-global/'>Ebola outbreak declared global emergency by WHO after 88 deaths in Congo</a> - theglobeandmail.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/ebola-in-afric